course: dictionary
lesson: 10922
title: The volt: measuring electric push
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A volt is the unit we use to measure voltage, the "push" that makes electricity flow around a circuit. The more volts, the harder the push. We write it in short form as V, so a 1.5 V battery has a push of 1.5 volts.
explain: A battery with more volts pushes harder, so a bulb connected to it glows brighter. Batteries and sockets have their voltage marked on them, so you can check the push before you use them.
explain: High voltage is dangerous. «Overhead railway wires carry about 25,000 volts, and the electricity can jump to you if you just get close.» A big push can hurt you without any touch, so never poke anything into a socket.
fact: A volt is the unit used to measure voltage, the push that makes electricity flow.
fact: The short form of volt is V, so 1.5 volts can be written as 1.5 V.
fact: An AA battery is a 1.5-volt cell, and putting more cells in a line gives more volts.
fact: Mains electricity is about 230 volts in the UK and about 120 volts in the USA.
fact: The volt is named after the Italian scientist Alessandro Volta, who made an early battery.
exercise: «Overhead railway wires carry about 25,000 volts.» What does volts measure here? | The number of trains on the line | *The push of the electricity | The weight of the wire | The length of the wire
exercise: A battery is marked 1.5 V. What does the V stand for? | *Volt, the unit of electric push | Vanilla | Velocity | Vase
exercise: Which has the biggest push? | *A mains socket in the UK, about 230 volts | A pencil | A bulb | A 1.5 V battery
test: More cells in a line give more volts. What happens to a bulb? | Turns into a buzzer | Changes colour | Goes dim | *Gets brighter
test: Who is the volt named after? | Marie Curie | Albert Einstein | Isaac Newton | *Alessandro Volta
exam: «Never go onto railway tracks; overhead wires can carry 25,000 volts and the electricity can jump to someone close by.» Why is this dangerous? | Because trains are very noisy | Because the wires are pretty and tempting | Because the tracks are slippery when wet | *Because high voltage can jump to you without a touch
